# 1. Reflection

The results reveal two distinct regulatory pathways for IFNG production: strong negative regulators (ZAP70, VAV1, CD3G, CD3D, CD3E, PTPRC with scores < -2.6) that appear to be TCR/immune checkpoint inhibitors, and positive regulators (NFKB2, CBLB, MAP4K1, TSC1 with scores > 1.0) that promote IFNG expression. The hits show that T cell receptor signaling components and their negative regulators are critical nodes. I should now focus on untested genes in the immediate signaling neighborhood of these validated hits—particularly genes in the TCR cascade, MAPK signaling downstream effectors, chromatin remodeling complexes that regulate IFNG, and metabolic regulators that support Th1 differentiation.

# 2. Research Plan

My strategy is to prioritize untested genes functionally connected to the strongest hits: (1) TCR signaling complex members and adaptor proteins that work with ZAP70, VAV1, and CD3 molecules; (2) downstream MAPK kinases and transcription factors in the JAK-STAT pathway that activate IFNG promoter; (3) chromatin modifiers and transcriptional coactivators (BRD family members, histone acetyltransferases) that regulate IFNG locus accessibility; (4) metabolic checkpoint kinases (mTOR pathway members, AMPK regulators) that control Th1 cell fate; (5) negative feedback regulators of TCR signaling (PTPN family phosphatases, ubiquitin ligases). This focused approach leverages the validated hits to find synergistic or epistatic interactions in the same pathways.

