# 1. Reflection

The data reveals strong functional pathways regulating IFNG production. Key observations: (1) TCR/CD3 signaling components (CD3E, CD3G, CD3D, ZAP70, LCK) show strong negative scores, suggesting inhibitory roles; (2) JAK-STAT pathway members (JAK1, JAK3, STAT3) are negative hits, indicating these are critical activators; (3) NF-κB pathway components (IKBKB, CHUK, IKBKG, RELA) are negative, confirming their importance; (4) Some positive hits (NFKB2, TSC1, CBLB, MAP4K1) suggest regulatory feedback loops; (5) IFNG itself shows strong negative score, indicating auto-suppression. The next round should focus on: upstream kinases activating JAK-STAT-NF-κB pathways, additional TCR signaling mediators, and epigenetic regulators of IFNG expression.

# 2. Research Plan

Target untested genes in three strategic areas: (1) upstream TCR-proximal kinases and adaptor proteins (SLP76, LAT, LYZ, GADS, PAG1 relatives) and additional Src-family kinases; (2) JAK-STAT pathway components not yet tested (STAT4, STAT5A, STAT5B already tested but need JAK2 pathway exploration, focusing on STAT co-activators and negative regulators); (3) NF-κB upstream kinases and IκB kinases not yet covered; (4) Chromatin remodelers and histone modifiers affecting IFNG locus accessibility; (5) IL-12/IL-18 signaling amplifiers and feedback suppressors. This systematic approach exploits the strong pathway-level signal already identified while filling gaps in our coverage of known IFNG regulatory networks.

