# 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 63 high-confidence hits with strong effects on IFNG production. Key observations: (1) Negative hits (LCK, ZAP70, VAV1, CD3E, CD3G, CD3D, PTPRC) suggest T cell receptor signaling suppresses IFNG, while positive hits (MAP4K1, CBLB, NFKB2, TSC1) suggest mTOR and NF-κB pathways promote it; (2) JAK-STAT signaling shows mixed effects (JAK1/JAK3 negative, STAT3 negative, but STAT1 tested as mild negative); (3) Several chromatin regulators (BRD4, KMT2A, CREBBP) and metabolic regulators (TSC1/TSC2, HIF1A) are hits, suggesting epigenetic and metabolic control; (4) The initial 128 genes tested provided a broad survey with modest effect sizes, allowing us to focus on validated pathways.

# 2. Research Plan

I will prioritize genes in three categories: (1) **Direct interactors of validated hits** - proteins that physically interact with strong hit genes (e.g., co-factors of JAK1, downstream of TSC1/mTOR, NF-κB complex members); (2) **Pathway components** - genes in parallel or upstream branches of TCR, JAK-STAT, mTOR/TSC, NF-κB, and chromatin remodeling pathways not yet tested; (3) **Epigenetic and metabolic regulators** - since BRD4, KMT2A, and metabolic genes showed effects, I'll test related readers, writers, erasers, and metabolic checkpoints. This focused approach leverages the validated pathway knowledge while maintaining coverage of untested nodes in known IFNG regulatory networks.

