## 1. Reflection
The screen has identified 63 high-confidence hits with strong effects on IFNG production. Key observations: (1) Strong negative regulators include ZAP70 (-3.92), VAV1 (-3.84), CD3G (-3.63), and CD3D (-3.31), suggesting T cell receptor signaling suppresses IFNG; (2) IFNG itself shows -1.69, indicating feedback inhibition; (3) Positive regulators include NFKB2 (+1.58), CBLB (+1.36), MAP4K1 (+1.23), TSC1 (+1.09), suggesting mTOR and NF-κB pathway activation promotes IFNG; (4) The first-round tested genes show modest effects (max ~0.38), indicating the second round successfully enriched for stronger hits. Next steps should focus on: identifying pathway partners of strong hits, testing genes in connected biological networks, and validating mechanism through regulatory relationships.

## 2. Research Plan
Based on strong hits suggesting T cell activation suppresses IFNG while NF-κB/mTOR signaling promotes it, I will prioritize: (1) direct interacting partners and upstream regulators of ZAP70, VAV1, and CD3 complex components; (2) additional NF-κB and mTOR pathway members not yet tested; (3) known IFNG transcriptional regulators (STAT1, IRF1, EOMES); (4) negative feedback regulators like SOCS family members and phosphatases; (5) related kinases and adaptor proteins in TCR and cytokine signaling. This network-based approach should identify additional strong hits by targeting functionally connected genes to validated regulators.

